Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Pearly translucent papule, Arborizing telangiectasias, Rolled border with central ulceration

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-008
Reason for Visit: Continuous, unprovoked bleeding from a chronic facial nodule causing significant patient distress.
Location: Nursing Home
Present at Visit: Patient, Registered Nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly, frail patient experiencing continuous, localized bleeding from a facial lesion that had not stopped with basic nursing interventions. Urgent assessment was required to achieve hemostasis, rule out an acutely bleeding malignant melanoma, and provide immediate wound management, preventing further blood loss and distress before the next routine GP visit.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time I dry my face with a towel, this lump on my nose bursts open and bleeds everywhere. Today it just won't stop."
History of Present Illness: The patient notes a slow, relentless, localized growth on the right nasal ala over the past two years. Initially presenting as a small pearly papule, it has slowly enlarged and developed central crusting. For the past 3 months, there has been intermittent bleeding following minor trauma or towel drying. Over the past 4 hours, the central crust detached spontaneously, resulting in continuous, slow oozing of blood that has soaked multiple tissues and caused considerable anxiety. The patient notes an absence of significant pain or pruritus associated with the lesion prior to this event.
Functional Status: Nursing home resident, requires assistance with ADLs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Frailty; anxiety related to continuous bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/76 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 61 Kg
General: Frail but alert, sitting in a wheelchair, holding a blood-stained gauze to the nose.
Physical Exam: Located on the chronically sun-exposed right nasal ala is a 1.2 cm pearly, translucent dome-shaped nodule. Upon careful cleaning of the clotted blood, raised and rolled pearly borders are visible. Prominent arborizing telangiectasias traverse the surface of the raised border. There is a 4mm central ulceration that is actively oozing venous-pattern blood. There is no surrounding induration, acute inflammatory erythema, or purulence. Palpation of the neck reveals a complete lack of palpable regional lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Standard nursing home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient is on low-dose Aspirin 100mg daily.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Nodulo-ulcerative Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC) with acute hemorrhage - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly pearly, translucent appearance and arborizing vessels, which favor BCC over the typically hyperkeratotic, crusty, and inflamed presentation of SCC.

Amelanotic Melanoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The very slow, relentless growth over years and classic rolled borders, lacking the rapid change and atypical vascularity of melanoma.

Pyogenic Granuloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronicity of the lesion (2 years); pyogenic granulomas typically erupt and grow rapidly over weeks.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acutely bleeding, centrally ulcerated nodule on a highly sun-exposed area, exhibiting the classic macroscopic features of a Basal Cell Carcinoma (BCC). Triage correctly identified the continuous bleeding in an elderly patient on antiplatelet therapy as requiring urgent after-hours intervention. The immediate clinical requirement was to provide definitive mechanical hemostasis that nursing staff were unable to achieve, address the patient's anxiety regarding the bleeding, and arrange a structured pathway for definitive diagnostic biopsy and excision. Delaying this intervention would have resulted in ongoing bleeding, wound contamination, and escalating psychological distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Achieved hemostasis utilizing firm direct pressure with alginate dressing and application of silver nitrate to the bleeding point. Secured a firm, non-adherent protective dressing.
Positive Behavioral Change: By keeping this thick dressing completely dry and untouched for the next 3 days, you will allow a strong clot to form over the fragile blood vessels, preventing another frightening bleeding episode. By patting your face gently with a towel rather than rubbing it, you will protect the delicate skin and stop the lump from breaking open again.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Instructed facility RNs on wound care and dressing changes.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1-2 weeks for definitive punch biopsy or referral for surgical excision.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the dressing becomes completely saturated with blood that drips continuously despite 20 minutes of firm, direct pressure, nursing staff must arrange transfer to the Emergency Department for surgical hemostasis.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The appearance is classic for a slow-growing skin cancer, but definitive diagnosis requires a laboratory biopsy.
Clinical Course: BCCs are locally destructive but very rarely spread internally. The bleeding is a mechanical issue due to fragile surface vessels, not a sign of rapid spread.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Apply firm, uninterrupted pressure for 20 minutes if bleeding restarts. Do not peek to see if it has stopped.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The silver nitrate chemically sealed the bleeding vessel, and the dressing provides a physical shield. The underlying lump needs to be removed eventually by your doctor to stop this from happening again.
Actionable Steps: Do not touch, scratch, or aggressively wash the area. Keep the dressing intact as instructed.

SMS INFORMATION

Assessed for bleeding facial nodule, likely Basal Cell Carcinoma. Hemostasis achieved with silver nitrate and dressing. Routine GP review needed for biopsy/excision.

SMS EDUCATION

Keep the facial dressing dry and intact for 3 days. Pat the face gently when drying. Apply 20 mins of firm pressure if bleeding restarts.
